Keeping fixup= and only skipping the boundary check for folds was
prototyped and worked in scratch tests; it still copies autosquash, so
it was not taken.

Tested in scratch repos with a minimal global config: fixup= fails with
the new message and no rebase starts; move= across a merge is still
refused; drop= and edit= work; the two-rebase fold of a commit on top
into a commit of a merged side branch leaves the tree identical, redoes
the merge and moves the note of the folded commit to the target.
